A||N||4||JOHN PANEK||ENGINE TEMPERATURE GAUGE||||5||
Z||000000||JOHN PANEK||11-15-2001||03:44 PM||||1987 Fiero GT 2.8L 153,000 Mi. - I'm baffled. I can't seem to get my engine temperature gauge to work. I've replaced the Temp Gauge Sending Unit as well as the Sensor but the gauge still won't come off 100 degrees. I checked the gauge by placing the ignition switch in the "ON" position and grounding the wires. One wire causes the lamp to go on and the other causes the gauge to move past max. hot. All of this leads me to believe that the dash gauge is o.k. I've switched the wires several times but still no response from the gauge. To further complicate things, my fuel gauge goes down to 1/2 from full when I turn the lights on....is this a clue? Any advise would be greatly appreciated.<P>(My speedometer doesn't work either but I'll tackle that one after I fixing this problem).||||reg||5
Z||000001||Brandon86SE||11-15-2001||03:47 PM||||K with that many problems in a localized area like that, My guess would be that you  have a bad ground for your panel.  That'd be the first thing I'd check.<P>Welcome to the forum by the way!<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rprevo||11-17-2001||03:08 PM|||| Behind the cluster is a green piece<BR>of plastic is has the contact wires in it.<BR>If memory serves correct the same 12 volts<BR>that feeds the speedo is the same one<BR>that feeds the Temp. Maybe that Green<BR>Plastic is damaged. Take the temp gage<BR>out of the cluster and look at the back<BR>of it you will see four leads the top lead<BR>should be ground and the other two that<BR>are across from each other should be the<BR>one for 12 volts and the other for the sender.<BR>At any rate when you know witch is witch<BR>you can bypass the cluster and hook the gage<BR>up to the battery direct and the temp sender<BR>direct right at the back of the gage. I went through this last winter when<BR>I put a Gt Package in my SE. You need a Haynes or a GM Manuel, I used a Haynes.<BR>It was pretty accurate for wire colors.<P> Well Good Luck!<P>  rprevo@ameritech.net<p>[This message has been edited by rprevo (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||6
